tical groups, broadening the scope of the repression documented here [Data: Entities (211, 212, 213, 214); Relationships (250, 251)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The site is linked to banned political affiliation and political persecution\",\"explanation\":\"MEMBRI AI UNOR PARTIDE INTERZISE are described as detainees who belonged to banned political parties such as PNȚ or PNL, and PNȚ is specifically named as one such party. This indicates that political affiliation was a basis for detention at the site. The presence of this relationship reinforces the interpretation of the prison as an instrument of political repression rather than ordinary incarceration alone [Data: Entities (214, 215, 210); Relationships (253)].\"},{\"summary\":\"A mass grave in the courtyard connects the prison to physical evidence of death\",\"explanation\":\"GROAPĂ COMUNĂ is described as a mass grave that existed in the courtyard of the building where the prison was located. The courtyard is directly linked to the building, and the grave is now associated with the area occupied by Parcul Trandafirilor. This adds a grave physical dimension to the community, indicating that the site was not only a place of detention and torture but also one associated with burial and death [Data: Entities (217, 218, 219, 200); Relationships (256, 257, 237)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Parcul Trandafirilor now occupies the former grave area\",\"explanation\":\"PARCUL TRANDAFIRILOR is the present-day park that occupies the area in the courtyard where the mass grave once existed. Its relationship to the Palace of Justice building shows how the site has been transformed from a place of repression into a public space. This modern overlay is important because it ties contemporary geography to the historical memory of the prison and grave [Data: Entities (200, 218, 219); Relationships (237, 256)].\"},{\"summary\":\"A memorial plaque and inscription preserve the site’s memory\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes a white marble memor